    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fa1e9c7_6e85c148",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/x86.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 10056,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-27T19:39:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was reading the code again and looks like the effect of these two events is basically setting `vcpu-\u003earch.mp_state` to `KVM_MP_STATE_INIT_RECEIVED` (and vcpu_reset hypercall) and then to `KVM_MP_STATE_RUNNABLE` (and sipi_deliver hypercall). So, I was wondering if we set this directly here to `KVM_MP_STATE_RUNNABLE`, would we be able to save couple of hypercalls? WDYT?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 10055,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 10056,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "fixSuggestions": [
        {
          "fixId": "badb3f87_eeaad69d",
          "description": "prompt_to_edit API",
          "replacements": [
            {
              "path": "arch/x86/kvm/x86.c",
              "range": {
                "startLine": 10049,
                "startChar": 0,
                "endLine": 10057,
                "endChar": 0
              },
              "replacement": "\t/* Reset the vCPU state as if an INIT was received. */\n\tkvm_vcpu_reset(vcpu, false);\n\n\t/* Directly set the state to RUNNABLE, bypassing the SIPI handling.\n\t * pKVM enforces the needed start vector, so the SIPI vector is not needed. */\n\tvcpu-\u003earch.mp_state \u003d KVM_MP_STATE_RUNNABLE;\n\n"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "revId": "a64618c1e5039f9380f21dfb8f5d73b03e83fa76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84698106_0662ec45",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/x86.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 10056,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-27T19:55:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AFAICT, KVM doesn\u0027t tend to just freely set mp_state of any vCPU on any CPU (not just on a CPU where that vCPU is loaded). We should be careful with races.\n\nAlso that\u0027s not the whole story. kvm_vcpu_reset(), among other things, calls kvm_lapic_reset(), and that part should be done by the host, since it is not done by pKVM (since LAPIC is emulated by the host, not by pKVM). We could just directly call kvm_lapic_reset() here too, but I\u0027m not sure that would be quite safe either.",
      "parentUuid": "1fa1e9c7_6e85c148",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 10055,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 10056,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "a64618c1e5039f9380f21dfb8f5d73b03e83fa76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9665c536_9959aecd",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/x86.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 10056,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-27T22:10:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "fair point. I had gone through the path from kicking the vcpu to entering the guest and seemed safe to set it, but just noticed what you mentioned - probably all mp_state manipulation happens with vcpu_get() in its path. So it might not be safe as you suggested. Resolving",
      "parentUuid": "84698106_0662ec45",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 10055,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 10056,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "a64618c1e5039f9380f21dfb8f5d73b03e83fa76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b2a7b90c_261fef06",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/x86.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 10056,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-28T00:06:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Besides that, since KVM-high still happens to be responsible for LAPIC emulation for the guest, and LAPIC is architecturally responsible for starting CPUs, it generally feels safer if this START_CPU hypercall implementation \"organically coexists\" with the existing KVM LAPIC implementation as is, not intrudes in the way how it works if not necessary.",
      "parentUuid": "9665c536_9959aecd",
      "range": {
